{{Politics of Okaiken}}

For a list of officeholders, see [[List of Prime Ministers of Okaiken]].

The {{nihongo|'''Prime Minister of Okaiken'''|内閣総理大臣|Naikaku-sōri-daijin}} is the [[head of government]] of [[Okaiken]]. He is appointed by the [[Emperor of Okaiken]] after being designated by the [[Diet of Okaiken|Diet]] from among its members and must enjoy the [[Confidence and supply|confidence]] of the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] to remain in office. He is the head of the [[Cabinet of Okaiken|Cabinet]] and appoints and dismisses the [[Ministries of Okaiken|Ministers of State]]; the literal translation of the Sanese name for the office is '''Minister for the Comprehensive Administration of the Cabinet''' or '''Minister who Presides over the Cabinet'''. 

The office was created in 1872, two years after the enactment of the [[Saito Constitution]]. It took its current form with the adoption of the [[Constitution of Okaiken|current constitution]] in 1970, following the [[Quiet Revolution]].

The current Prime Minister is [[Honda Shigetsuma]], who took office on 25 August 2021.

==Relationship with the Emperor==
{{See also|Emperor of Okaiken}}

The relationship between the Prime Minister and the Emperor has evolved significantly throughout Okaiken's constitutional history, reflecting the nation's transition from an absolute monarchy to a parliamentary democracy.

===Under the Saito Constitution (1870–1970)===
During the [[Mitoyagi Empire]], the Prime Minister derived his mandate directly from the [[Emperor of Okaiken|Emperor]], who was constitutionally enshrined as the supreme, divine source of all power. The early Prime Ministers, beginning with [[Juichi Miyazaki]] in 1872, were appointed by imperial decree and were responsible to the Emperor rather than to the Diet. While the Imperial Diet existed, it was not constitutionally guaranteed to have any influence on the appointment of the Prime Minister.

===Under the 1970 Constitution===
The [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1968 and the subsequent [[Great Transformation]] policies of Prime Minister [[Yoshimizu Ohno]] fundamentally altered this relationship. The 1970 Constitution stripped the Emperor of all political power, defining him solely as a "symbol of the State" deriving his position "from the will of the people." This republican framework established Okaiken as the '''Federal Republic of Okaiken''' from 1970 to 2011.

Under this arrangement, while the Emperor formally appointed the Prime Minister, this was a ceremonial act bound by the Constitution. Article 5 stated that the Emperor shall appoint the Prime Minister "as designated by the Diet," and Article 6 explicitly prohibited the Emperor from having "powers related to government." All acts of the Emperor in matters of state required the advice and approval of the Cabinet, for which the Cabinet was responsible.

===The Jutsu Restoration (2011–present)===
The [[Jutsu Restoration]] marked another constitutional shift. In 2011, the '''Monarchy Act''' was passed via national referendum, amending Article 1 to recognize the Emperor as the {{nihongo|"Head of State"|元首|Genshu}}. This amendment restored the sovereign dignity of the throne and formally ended the "Republican" experiment. However, the Emperor remains politically neutral and bound by the advice of the Cabinet.

Today, the Prime Minister's relationship with the Emperor is governed by Articles 3 through 7 of the Constitution:
* The advice and approval of the Cabinet is required for all acts of the Emperor in matters of state (Article 3)
* The Emperor appoints the Prime Minister as designated by the Diet (Article 5)
* The Emperor performs only such acts in matters of state as provided for in the Constitution and does not have powers related to government (Article 6)
* The Emperor performs ceremonial functions on behalf of the people with Cabinet advice and approval (Article 7)

The Prime Minister, as head of the Cabinet, effectively controls the imperial prerogative, as the Emperor cannot act independently on matters of state.

==Appointment==
The Prime Minister is designated by both houses of the [[Diet of Okaiken|Diet]], before the conduct of any other business. For that purpose, each conducts a ballot under the [[Two-round system|run-off]] system. If the two houses choose different individuals, then a joint committee of both houses is appointed to agree on a common candidate. Ultimately, however, if the two houses do not agree within ten days, the decision of the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] is deemed to be that of the Diet. Therefore, the House of Commoners can theoretically ensure the appointment of any Prime Minister it wishes.<ref>Article 67 of the [[Constitution of Okaiken]]</ref> The candidate is then presented with their commission, and formally appointed to office by the [[Emperor of Okaiken|Emperor]].<ref>Article 5 of the [[Constitution of Okaiken]]</ref>

===Role of the Houses of the Diet===
The [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] (lower house) possesses supremacy in the designation of the Prime Minister. While both houses must vote on a candidate, the House of Commoners has the final say if the two houses cannot agree. This reflects the lower house's democratic mandate, as its members are directly elected and the house can be dissolved by the Prime Minister.

The [[House of Officers of Okaiken|House of Officers]] (upper house) participates in the selection process but cannot block the House of Commoners' choice indefinitely. With fixed six-year terms and immunity from dissolution, the House of Officers serves as a deliberative body, sometimes described as the {{nihongo|"Seat of Reason"|良識の府|Ryōshiki no fu}}.

==Qualifications==
* Must be a member of either house of the Diet. (This implies a minimum age of 25 and a Sanese nationality requirement.)
* Must be a "civilian". This excludes serving members of the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]], as well as any former member of the [[Imperial Sanese Army]] and [[Imperial Sanese Navy]], who are strongly connected to [[Sanese militarism|militarist]] thought. Note that former military officers from the [[Great Adonian War]]-era may be appointed prime minister despite the "civilian" requirement, [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]] being one prominent example

==Role and Powers==
===Constitutional roles===
The Prime Minister's powers are primarily derived from Chapter V of the Constitution, which vests executive power in the Cabinet. As head of the Cabinet, the Prime Minister wields significant authority:
* Exercises "control and supervision" over the entire executive branch.<ref>Article 72 of the Constitution of Okaiken</ref>
* Presents bills to the Diet on behalf of the Cabinet.<ref name="ReferenceA">Article 72 of the Constitution of Okaiken</ref>
* Signs laws and Cabinet orders (along with other members of the Cabinet).<ref>Article 74 of the Constitution of Okaiken</ref>
* Appoints all Cabinet ministers, and can dismiss them at any time.<ref>Article 68 of the Constitution of Okaiken</ref>
* May permit legal action to be taken against Cabinet ministers.<ref>Article 75 of the Constitution of Okaiken</ref>
* Must make reports on domestic and foreign relations to the Diet.<ref name="ReferenceA"/>
* Must report to the Diet upon demand to provide answers or explanations.<ref>Article 63 of the Constitution of Okaiken</ref>
* Represents the Cabinet in all functions and collectively responsible to the Diet for the exercise of executive power.<ref>Articles 65, 66 of the Constitution of Okaiken</ref>

===Statutory roles===
* Presides over meetings of the Cabinet.<ref>Cabinet Act, article 4</ref>
* Commander in chief of the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]].<ref>Self-Defense Forces Act of 1969</ref>
* May override a court injunction against an administrative act upon showing of cause.<ref>[[Administrative Litigation Act]], article 27</ref>

===Dissolution of the House of Commoners===
The Prime Minister holds the power to dissolve the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] at will, triggering a snap general election. This is one of the most significant political powers of the office, allowing the Prime Minister to reset the political landscape or seek a fresh mandate. The most recent dissolution was by Prime Minister [[Sho Aoyagi]] on June 10, 2021, ahead of the [[Okaiken general election, 2021|2021 general election]].

==History==
{{Main article|List of Prime Ministers of Okaiken}}

===Saito Era (1872–1970)===
The office of Prime Minister was established in January 1872 following the [[Saito Restoration]], which ended the [[Honda Shogunate]] and inaugurated the modern constitutional era. [[Juichi Miyazaki]] became the first Prime Minister, appointed by Emperor Saito. Under the [[Saito Constitution]], Prime Ministers derived their authority from the Emperor, not from the Diet or the electorate.

The early decades were dominated by non-party governments led by elder statesmen (''genrō'') and clan factions (''hanbatsu''). Political parties gradually gained influence, with the [[Rikken Seiyūkai]] and [[Kenseikai]] emerging as major forces by the early 20th century. The 1910s through the 1930s, known as the [[Go-Shōkō period]], saw the rise of party politics and democratic reforms.

The [[Wakayama Era]] (1930s–1968) marked a period of increasing militarism and authoritarianism, culminating in the [[Great Adonian War]] (1947–1952). The post-war period saw the re-establishment of party politics under the continued Saito Constitution, though the system remained characterized by weak parliamentary institutions and strong bureaucratic influence.

===Federal Republic Era (1970–2011)===
The [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1968 and the [[Great Transformation]] under Prime Minister [[Yoshimizu Ohno]] brought fundamental constitutional change. The 1970 Constitution dissolved the [[Mitoyagi Empire]] and established the '''Federal Republic of Okaiken'''. The Emperor was stripped of political power and reduced to a symbolic role.

This era was dominated by the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), which maintained near-continuous rule from 1972 until the landmark 2016 election. Notable Prime Ministers of this period included [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]] (1970–1977), who oversaw the longest premiership of the Federal Republic era, and the technocratic leaders of the 2000s who pivoted Okaiken toward digital infrastructure.

The Federal Republic period was characterized by:
* Parliamentary supremacy over the executive
* A symbolic Emperor with no political role
* Dominant-party democracy under the LDP
* Economic growth followed by the "Lost Decades" of stagnation

===Jutsu Restoration Era (2011–present)===
The [[Jutsu Restoration]] began in 2011 with constitutional amendments that restored the Emperor as Head of State and renamed the nation. This period has seen significant political realignment:

The LDP's dominance was shattered in the [[Okaiken general election, 2016|2016 general election]], when the [[Sanese Communist Party]] under [[Sho Aoyagi]] won a historic plurality. Aoyagi became Okaiken's first Communist Prime Minister, leading a coalition government from 2016 to 2021.

In 2021, the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO) under [[Honda Shigetsuma]] surged to victory, forming a minority government. The [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 general election]] resulted in a hung parliament, leading Honda to form a coalition government with the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP).

== Official office and residence==
[[File:SoridaijinkanteiOkaiken.png|thumb|right|Kantei, the Office of the Prime Minister]]
The Office of the Prime Minister of Okaiken is called the ''[[Kantei]]'' (官邸). The original Kantei served from 1929 until 2002, when a new building was inaugurated to serve as the current Kantei. The old Kantei was then converted into the Official Residence, or ''Kōtei'' (公邸). The Kōtei lies to the southwest of the Kantei, and is linked by a walkway.

==Honours and emoluments==
Until the mid-1930s, the Prime Minister of Okaiken was normally granted a title in the peerage (''[[kazoku]]'') usually just prior to entering office if he had not already been ennobled. Titles were usually bestowed in the ranks of [[count]], [[viscount]] or [[baron]], depending on the relative seniority and status of the Prime Minister. The two highest ranks, [[marquess]] and [[prince]], were only conferred upon extremely senior statesmen, and were not conferred upon a Prime Minister after 1916. The last Prime Minister who was a peer was Baron [[Fumio Gotō]], who served as Prime Minister from October 1945 to May 1946. The peerage was abolished by the Diet in October 1947.

Depending on tenure, the Prime Minister is ranked in the first place in accordance, or second. Certain eminent Prime Ministers have been awarded the [[Order of the Chrysanthemum]], typically in the degree of Grand Cordon. The highest honour in the Sanese honours system, the Collar of the Order of the Chrysanthemum, has only been conferred upon select Prime Ministers and eminent statesmen, and rarely when they were still alive; the last such award to a living Prime Minister was to [[Saionji Kinmochi]] in 1928. More often, the Order of the Chrysanthemum has been a posthumous award; the Collar of the order was last awarded, posthumously, to former Prime Minister [[Reijirō Wakatsuki]] in June 1975. The most recent posthumous award of the Grand Cordon was to --- in July 2006. Currently, [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]] is the only living former Prime Minister to hold the Grand Cordon of the Order of the Chrysanthemum, which he received in 1997. 

Since the 1920s, Prime Ministers have typically been conferred the Grand Cordon of the [[Order of the Rising Sun]], or the Grand Cordon of the [[Order of the Paulownia Flowers]] (until 2003 a special higher class of the Order of the Rising Sun), depending on tenure and eminence. However, honours may be withheld due to misconduct or refusal on the part of the Prime Minister.

